WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.160
React Native is now a serious, high-performance production choice

2
00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:09.600
for building complex, dual-platform mobile applications in the year 2026.

3
00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:14.960
This academy is designed for engineers who already know how to build and ship software.

4
00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:19.760
Absolute coding beginners will find the pace is optimized for experienced developers.

5
00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:23.840
You'll focus on migrating your current expertise into the new architecture,

6
00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:27.360
skipping the introductory programming concepts you've already mastered.

7
00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:32.880
To achieve that, this course offers four distinct trails that converge on one single destination.

8
00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:36.720
Auditing your own skills to select the correct starting path

9
00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:39.760
ensures you leverage your existing strengths immediately,

10
00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:42.320
rather than repeating basic tutorials.

11
00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:46.480
If you jump into a path that assumes deep native knowledge you don't possess,

12
00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:48.000
you will hit a wall immediately.

13
00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:52.080
Conversely, developers who have been shipping front-end features for years

14
00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:55.440
will find standard state management tutorials a poor use of time.

15
00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:58.640
You end up paying a heavy translation tax,

16
00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:02.080
burning mental energy trying to map your everyday coding workflow

17
00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:03.840
to completely foreign terminology.

18
00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:08.240
We're going to run a quick, objective audit of your current stack right now

19
00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:09.840
to match you to your optimal trail.

20
00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:14.320
Mastering React Native quickly depends on bypassing redundant lessons

21
00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:17.280
and focusing on the platform gaps you actually have.

22
00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:20.640
If you live in the browser, writing React components with hooks,

23
00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:24.560
structuring JSX, and managing props, you belong on the web trail.

24
00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:27.760
All of the component logic you write every day for the web

25
00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:30.240
maps cleanly onto mobile UI architecture.

26
00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:33.520
This diagram shows how you'll adapt to mobile layout logic.

27
00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:38.080
You'll swap standard CSS for React Native's strict flexbox engine

28
00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:39.360
to position every element.

29
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:44.640
You'll also trade standard browser APIs for platform-specific native modules,

30
00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:47.760
connecting your JavaScript directly to the device's camera,

31
00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:49.680
location services, and local storage.

32
00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:53.920
The good news is your existing knowledge of state management transfers perfectly.

33
00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:56.560
It requires zero modification.

34
00:01:57.120 --> 00:01:59.840
This route acts as a highly efficient bridge,

35
00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:02.960
allowing front-end developers to achieve native mobile capability

36
00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:05.440
without ever leaving the JavaScript ecosystem.

37
00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:08.960
If you build for Android, specifically writing Kotlin

38
00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:12.400
and actively using Jetpack Compose, you take the Android trail.

39
00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:17.040
Looking at this timeline comparison, you'll see exactly how the life cycles

40
00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:22.240
of activities and fragments map conceptually straight into React Native's state management.

41
00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:26.880
But if you lack Kotlin or Compose knowledge, these comparative analyses

42
00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:29.680
will only confuse you rather than clarify the concepts.

43
00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:35.040
Over on the Apple side, the iOS trail requires a strict baseline of Swift

44
00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:36.320
and modern Swift UI.

45
00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:40.480
We will use your working knowledge of the app delegate and view controllers

46
00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:43.840
to explain how React navigation handles routing behind the scenes.

47
00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:48.240
Keep in mind, legacy Objective-C backgrounds are excluded here.

48
00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:52.320
Modern Swift UI knowledge is mandatory to benefit from this specific curriculum.

49
00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:57.440
The shared advantage for both of these native paths is learning cross-platform deployment

50
00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:00.160
while respecting the native life cycles you already understand.

51
00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:04.560
Native analyses accelerate your learning if they match your daily work,

52
00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:07.920
but they are actively detrimental if you lack that specific background.

53
00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:11.120
Our fourth option is the master class.

54
00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:14.240
This is designed exclusively for developers who already possess

55
00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:15.920
fundamental React Native experience.

56
00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:19.440
This trail bypasses basic UI creation entirely,

57
00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:22.480
focusing purely on implementing the new architecture

58
00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:24.640
and reading and writing fluent TypeScript.

59
00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:26.400
Cautionary.

60
00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:29.600
Attempting this path without strong fundamentals is a mistake.

61
00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:32.720
It will leave you with critical gaps in your understanding of core APIs.

62
00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:37.440
Regardless of the path you select, you will use a universal execution method

63
00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:38.720
for the initial exercises.

64
00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:41.680
This interface represents Expo Snack.

65
00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:45.120
It provides a frictionless testing environment right in your browser.

66
00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:48.880
You can run every code example instantly to verify your logic,

67
00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:52.080
without needing to configure simulators or run terminal commands.

68
00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:55.200
While Expo Snack removes the initial friction,

69
00:03:55.200 --> 00:04:00.480
you will eventually migrate to a local setup to compile the final dual-platform deployments.

70
00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:03.120
The browser tools get you writing code on day one,

71
00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:07.200
but rigorous local production readiness remains the ultimate goal for every trail.

72
00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:12.560
Regardless of your starting point, all four trails lead to identical fluency

73
00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:14.960
in TypeScript, Core Components, and Flexbox.

74
00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:18.480
This diagnostic decision tree finalizes your choice

75
00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:21.040
based strictly on your daily driver language.

76
00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:23.120
If you build with React and JavaScript,

77
00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:25.760
follow the logic flow directly to the web trail.

78
00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:28.640
If your background is in Kotlin and Jetpack Compose,

79
00:04:28.640 --> 00:04:30.720
map your way to the Android trail.

80
00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:35.200
Swift and SwiftUI developers follow the flow down to the iOS trail.

81
00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:38.000
And if you already have existing React Native experience,

82
00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:40.320
you bypass the basics and head to the master class.

83
00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:45.120
If you are an absolute beginner or possess zero of the matching prerequisites,

84
00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:46.160
you will land here.

85
00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:50.320
This blueprint aligns the curriculum with your specific background,

86
00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:54.560
ensuring you spend your time on new concepts rather than reviewing familiar territory.

87
00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:57.360
Here is the final reality check.

88
00:04:57.360 --> 00:05:00.080
If no prerequisites are met, step away.

89
00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:03.680
Build your foundational software skills first and return when you are ready.

90
00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:07.200
For everyone else, your immediate next step in the curriculum

91
00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:09.040
is the History and Architecture module.

92
00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:12.880
Understanding exactly why React Native is compelling today

93
00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:16.880
provides the final required context before you begin your specialized trail.

